Add a button size option to the window decoration

The KDecoration3 decoration takes the button side from the title bar alone.
buttonSize() in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecoration.cpp gives the height of the
title bar, less the gap above a button and the gap below it. That height comes
from the caption font and from the TitleBarPad key of the kwinqtcurverc file. A
user who wants a larger button must therefore raise the font or the pad, and
both moves change the caption as well. The settings module of the decoration
names no button size, and neither does the KWin page of the QtCurve style. The
Breeze decoration gives such an option today.

The patch adds the ButtonSize key to the General group of the kwinqtcurverc
file. DecorationConfig in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecorationconfig.h gains the
ButtonSize enumeration, the buttonSize() reader and the name table of the key.
The five names are the names of kcm_breezedecoration: ButtonTiny, ButtonSmall,
ButtonDefault, ButtonLarge and ButtonVeryLarge. readButtonSize() gives an absent
key and an unknown name the default size, as readShade() gives an unknown shade
the no shade value.

Decoration::targetButtonSize() in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecoration.cpp holds
the size math. Each size takes a multiple of the grid unit. The five
multiples are one, one and one half, two, two and one half, and three and one
half. buttonSize() in kdecoration/breezedecoration.cpp of Breeze uses the same
five multiples. KDecoration3::DecorationSettings::gridUnit() reports the unit in
pixels, and that unit follows the caption font. The old default side of the
caption font no longer sets any button size.

The decoration takes each product to the nearest odd number of pixels, so a
glyph keeps a centre pixel. A product at the same distance from two odd numbers
takes the larger one. The side of Breeze differs from the side here, because
Breeze draws the bare product. At a grid unit of 10 pixels Breeze draws 10, 15,
20, 25 and 35 pixels. This decoration draws 11, 15, 21, 25 and 35 pixels.

minButtonSide in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecoration.cpp holds every side at 11
pixels or more. The decoration drew no button under 11 pixels before this patch.
A button of 7 pixels or less changes the line art. Button::paint() gives such a
button a line art of 3 pixels or less. drawGlyph() then draws each stroke 1
pixel wide, and not 2 pixels wide.

The order of the five sizes never falls: Tiny, Small, Normal, Large, Very Large.
The floor is the only cause of a tie in that order. The Tiny size and the Small
size tie at a grid unit of 7 pixels or less. A smaller grid unit ties more
sizes.

titleBarHeight() takes the larger of the default title bar height and the height
that the button needs, as bordersFor() of Breeze does. defaultTitleBarHeight()
adds the caption font height, the room for the caption shadow and the
TitleBarPad value at the top and at the bottom. The larger sizes therefore raise
the title bar, and updateButtonsGeometry() centres a smaller button in that bar.
The TitleBarPad key still grows the title bar, and it leaves every button side
as it is.

The default render changes, and the change is deliberate. The Normal size now
takes two grid units. The previous patch took the title bar height, less the
gap above the button and the gap below it. At DejaVu Sans 10 pt with no pad the
close button goes from 15 pixels to 21 pixels. The title bar goes from 21
pixels to 27 pixels. An unchanged kwinqtcurverc file therefore draws a changed
window.

Decoration::init() connects the fontChanged signal of the settings object to
updateButtonsGeometryDelayed(). The grid unit follows the caption font, and the
title bar does not always follow it. A font change can leave every border as it
is, and the bordersChanged signal then stays silent. The connection moves the
buttons in that case.

minGlyphSide in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecorationbutton.cpp now holds 1 pixel,
which is the smallest side that draws. The rule of Button::paint() then sets the
line art alone. That rule takes three sevenths of the button side. It rounds
that number up to a whole number of pixels, and it then adds 1 pixel to an even
number. The old floor of 5 pixels never raised the line art at a button side of
11 pixels or more.

Both rounding steps go up. The previous patch rounded to the nearest whole
number of pixels, and it took an even result down. That rule gives a button of
11 pixels and a button of 15 pixels the same line art of 5 pixels. Those two
sides are the Tiny size and the Small size at a grid unit of 10 pixels. The
round up gives the Small size 7 pixels of line art, and it leaves the Tiny size
at 5 pixels. Each result stays odd, so the line art keeps a centre pixel.

The line art keeps the order of the five sizes, and two entries can still take
the same value. At DejaVu Sans 8 pt the five sides are 11, 13, 17, 21 and 29
pixels. The line art there is 5, 7, 9, 9 and 13 pixels. The Normal size and the
Large size therefore tie at that font. The tie comes from the odd line art of a
small button, and not from the floor.

The question mark of the context help button now takes a round join at a small
button. It takes the miter join of every other glyph at a large one. Two corners
of the mark are sharp. The corner at rect.right() throws a miter spike to the
right, and the apex corner at the centre of the top edge throws one upward. The
spike runs 3 pixels past the right edge of the line art, and 2 pixels above the
top edge.

helpRoundJoinMargin in qt6/kdecoration/qtcurvedecorationbutton.cpp holds the
rule. paint() gives the margin as half of the button side less the line art
side. drawGlyph() takes the round join while that margin is 4 pixels or less.
The rule therefore takes the round join at a button side of 11, 13, 15 and 17
pixels. It takes the miter join at every larger side. A round join holds each
spike to 1 pixel, and no pixel of the mark then falls on the ring.

A control build that always miters puts 3 pixels of the mark on the ring at a
side of 11 or 13 pixels. It puts 1 pixel there at a side of 15 or 17 pixels.
A build of the old rounding rule puts 3 pixels of the mark on the ring at a
side of 11 pixels. It puts 1 pixel there at a side of 13 pixels. The round up
widened the line art, and it raised the contact at the last three of those four
sides.

The settings module gains the row. DecorationSettings in
qt6/kdecoration/config/qtcurvedecorationsettings.h gains the buttonSize member,
and defaults(), load(), save() and operator== all hold it. save() writes the
name of the size, and not the number.

The combination box "Button size:" sits on the General tab, under the "Borders:"
row. It holds five entries in the order of kcm_breezedecoration. Breeze names
the middle entry Medium, and this module names it Normal. The help text of the
box states the multiple of each entry, and it states the floor of 11 pixels. The
Defaults button resets the box to Normal. A save asks KWin to reload its
configuration, so every open window changes at once without a restart.

The patch rewrites the help text of the "Titlebar text padding:" box in two
files. qt6/kdecoration/config/ui/qtcurvedecorationconfigui.ui holds the copy of
the settings module, and qt6/kwinconfig/qtcurvekwinconfigwidget.ui holds the
copy of the KWin page of the style dialog. The new sentence states that the pad
changes the height of the title bar, and not the buttons.

These settings stay outside the patch:

* a size for one button alone.
* a size for the window icon. paintTitleBar() keeps the caption font as the cap
  of that icon.
* the title alignment option of Breeze.

What the tests show:

* The golden report of the harness differs from the report of the previous patch
  in two places. The size of the two plugin files changed, and the four probe
  lines changed. Every other line matches, and the probe lines carry the new
  default render.
* DejaVu Sans at 8 pt, the five sizes in order: button side 11, 13, 17, 21 and
  29 pixels. The title bar height is 17, 19, 23, 27 and 35 pixels.
* DejaVu Sans at 10 pt, the five sizes in order: button side 11, 15, 21, 25 and
  35 pixels. The title bar height is 21, 21, 27, 31 and 41 pixels.
* DejaVu Sans at 14 pt, the five sizes in order: button side 15, 21, 29, 35 and
  49 pixels. The title bar height is 27, 27, 35, 41 and 55 pixels.
* The line art at DejaVu Sans 8 pt, the five sizes in order: 5, 7, 9, 9 and 13
  pixels. The Normal size and the Large size tie at that font. The sides of 17
  pixels and 21 pixels both take 9 pixels of odd line art.
* The line art at DejaVu Sans 10 pt, the five sizes in order: 5, 7, 9, 11 and 15
  pixels. The previous patch rounded to the nearest whole number of pixels, and
  it took an even result down. That rule gives 5, 5, 9, 11 and 15 pixels there,
  so the Small size is the one entry that changes.
* The line art at DejaVu Sans 14 pt, the five sizes in order: 7, 9, 13, 15 and
  21 pixels. The previous patch rounded to the nearest whole number of pixels,
  and it took an even result down. That rule gives 5, 9, 11, 15 and 21 pixels
  there.
* The measurement of the line art takes the width of the bar of the minimize
  button. That bar fills the line art box on the horizontal axis. Its width
  matches the rule at every entry of the three ladders.
* The side sweep runs the caption font DejaVu Sans from 5 pt to 40 pt, in steps
  of one half point. It takes each of the five sizes. The sweep gives 43 button
  sides, from 11 pixels to 141 pixels. The ButtonSize option sets no ceiling,
  because the button side follows the caption font. Every side count of this
  patch comes from that one sweep.
* No pixel of the line art falls on the frame ring in the default state. That
  state holds a button that is not pressed and carries no pointer. The check
  covered nine button types, at every one of the 43 button sides of the sweep.
  Those types are close, maximize, minimize, menu, application menu, shade,
  context help, keep above and keep below.
* The ring test sets titlebarAppearance, titlebarButtonAppearance and
  sunkenAppearance to flat. The fill of a button then matches the fill of the
  title bar, and a difference of two renders holds line work alone. That
  precondition carries the method, because drawFrame() paints a gradient under
  the border. A difference without the precondition holds the whole face of the
  button.
* The ring is the set of pixels that a render of the frame alone changes
  against a render with no button. The line art is the set of pixels that a
  render with no frame changes against the same render with no button. Every
  ring figure of this patch comes from that one method.
* A press and a hover put ink on the ring at a small button side. The
  measurement of a state adds one render of that state. It takes the ink of
  that state against the same render with no button.
* The close button covers 7 pixels of the ring at a side of 11 pixels, and 9
  pixels at a side of 13 pixels. It covers 2 pixels at a side of 15 pixels, and
  4 pixels at a side of 17 pixels. Every other button covers 2 pixels or less,
  at a side of 19 pixels or less. No button reaches the ring at a side of 21
  pixels or more.
* The help line art matches a build that takes the miter join at every side.
  That match is byte for byte, at 39 of the 43 sides of the sweep. Those 39
  sides run from 19 pixels to 141 pixels. The two builds differ at a side of 11,
  13, 15 and 17 pixels alone.
* The help line art matches a build of the old rounding rule, byte for byte, at
  14 of the 43 sides of the sweep. Those sides are 21, 25, 35, 39, 49, 53, 63,
  77, 81, 91, 95, 105, 119 and 133 pixels. Each of them is a side where the old
  rule gives the same line art side, and where the margin is over 4 pixels. The
  line art side and the join style are the only causes of a difference at any
  other side.
* TitleBarPad=10 at 10 pt gives the same five button sides as no pad: 11, 15,
  21, 25 and 35 pixels. The title bar height is 41 pixels at every size.
* TitleBarPad=-5 at 10 pt gives the same five button sides. The title bar
  height is 17, 21, 27, 31 and 41 pixels. The pad changes no button side at
  either value.
* The Breeze plugin at 10 pt gives 10, 15, 20, 25 and 35 pixels for the same
  five entries. The odd rule makes the two ladders differ at the Tiny entry and
  at the Normal entry.
* Every side is odd, and every button sits at the centre of the title bar.
* A probe changes the caption font of the settings object after init(), from
  15 pt to 16 pt, with ButtonSize=ButtonTiny and TitleBarPad=10. The title bar
  stays at 49 pixels, so no border moves. The button side goes from 15 pixels to
  17 pixels, and every button rectangle matches a fresh run at 16 pt.
* The build of the decoration and of the settings module gives no warning at
  -Wall -Wextra.

The change sits on the branch work/kdecoration3 of the local tree, as the third
commit. Merge request !9 on system/qtcurve holds that branch. The merge request
takes the third commit at the next push of the branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

